# REGRESSION PIN — the SECOND FACE of the `&(*b).<n>` tuple-field-through-a-Box
# gap, and the run-level pin for `register_collection_alias`.
#
# WRONG BEFORE THE FIX (measured at pristine HEAD): `gg build` rc 1 on BOTH
# backends — the mangled tuple type is referenced by the emitted code but never
# emitted, because the `Box` pre-pass cached `Box__…` with `_0: Unit` from the
# NON-registering mapper and the faithful mapper then found it cached. Read site
# faithful, writer one layer up lossy (Core #1).
#
# ⚠ THIS SHAPE EXISTS BECAUSE THE ONE FILED AS THE SECOND FACE DID NOT
# REPRODUCE. The filing spelled it `Box[(int, int)]` + `&(*b).1` — a PRIMITIVE
# tuple field — and that program is GREEN at pristine HEAD, so it pinned
# nothing. The discriminating axis is a NON-PRIMITIVE element in the tuple: with
# a struct in the first slot the mangled tuple type is the one that goes
# unemitted. No `Result` and no propagation anywhere in this program, which is
# what separates it from its sibling `sound_amp_box_tuple_field_cc_fail.gg`.
#
# Verified by reverting `register_collection_alias` ALONE: rc 1 returns on both
# backends while every other cell in the set stays green.
struct P:
    int x

void take(int &v):
    print(v)
    v = v + 1

void main():
    Box[(P, int)] b = Box((P(5), 7))
    take(&(*b).0.x)
    print("done")
